  "profile": {
    "video_id": "kenji:bN_QNjRprbU",
    "purpose": "Demonstrates cooking eggs in a cup with an espresso machine's steam wand, a technique Kenji saw at a cafe in Salamina, Colombia, then tries variations with sausage and a chawanmushi-style version with mushrooms and dashi.",
    "video_type": "how_to",
    "thesis": "An espresso machine's steam wand cooks eggs quickly into a uniquely light, custardy, fully set texture you can't get from a pan, and it's safe and easy to clean.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "Put a couple of eggs, butter and a pinch of salt in a cup, insert the preheated steam wand and agitate until the eggs set at about 160 to 170°F.",
      "The steam adds moisture and air, giving eggs that are fully set yet custardy and creamy.",
      "Cleaning the wand is no harder than cleaning milk proteins off it, and outward steam pressure keeps egg from going up inside the wand; purging is optional.",
      "Add-ins like Japanese arabiki sausage work well, as at the Colombian cafe where customers can request mix-ins.",
      "The result differs from chawanmushi, which has roughly a 5:1 dashi-to-egg ratio and is steamed in a steamer, but a chawanmushi-style version with sautéed beech mushrooms, dashi and chives works well.",
      "Dashi can be made with hot water straight from the espresso machine's water wand.",
      "The whole process takes well under a minute with virtually no cleanup."
    ],
